  "prompt": "Feature: add an image from another website to a recipe as an asset (the recipe page will let users drag an image from a web page onto it).\n\n1. A new endpoint POST /api/recipes/{slug}/assets/url, taking {\"url\": \"<image URL>\"} (the body the scrape endpoints take), for logged-in users only (401 otherwise). It downloads the image on the server and stores it as a recipe asset, the way POST /api/recipes/{slug}/assets stores an uploaded file (the same file naming, directory and allowed extensions, the asset recorded on the recipe), with the icon \"mdi-file-image\", and returns the asset.\n   - The asset's name is the URL's file name without extension or query string: \".../photos/pancakes.png?v=2\" gives \"pancakes\" (file name \"pancakes.png\"), \".../download?id=123\" gives \"download\". A URL without a file name, or with one that slugifies to nothing (\"https://example.test/$.png\"), gives \"image\".\n   - The file's extension comes from the response's content type, not from the URL. An extension the asset upload does not allow is 400, an SVG (\"svg+xml\", which browsers execute) among them, and nothing is written.\n   - The download is capped at 5 MB, held in ASSET_MAX_DOWNLOAD_BYTES, a module-level constant beside the existing asset upload route: the bytes are stored as they are, so time alone must not bound them.\n   - A download that is not an image, comes from a domain that is not allowed, is over the cap, or yields nothing is 400, with nothing written.\n2. RecipeDataService gets an async fetch_image(image_url, max_bytes=None) returning (bytes, extension) or None:\n   - it downloads with safehttp.resilient_fetch(image_url, allow_flaresolverr=False, max_bytes=max_bytes): FlareSolverr returns HTML, never image bytes;\n   - the extension is the content type's subtype, lower-cased, parameters dropped (\"IMAGE/JPEG; charset=binary\" gives \"jpeg\", \"image/png\" gives \"png\");\n   - a content type that is not image/* raises NotAnImageError, including one that merely contains \"image\" (\"text/html; name=image.png\") and a missing one;\n   - safehttp's InvalidDomainError is raised again as the service module's own InvalidDomainError; safehttp.ResponseTooLargeError passes through unchanged; any other exception is logged and gives None, as does a fetch that returns None;\n   - scrape_image uses fetch_image without a cap and writes the image with the extension fetch_image returned, instead of guessing it from the URL (the guess made every image a \"jpg\"). Its handling of a dict ({\"url\": ...}), of a list (the largest, by largest_content_len) and of input it cannot read is unchanged.\n3. safehttp gets ResponseTooLargeError (exported by the package) and resilient_fetch a max_bytes=None parameter. With max_bytes set, a response whose Content-Length declares more is refused before its body is read (a message containing \"declared content-length\"), and a body that grows past max_bytes while it is read is refused (a message containing \"response body exceeds\"), whatever its Content-Length said. Either refusal ends the fetch: another browser impersonation would not make the body smaller. Without max_bytes nothing is capped, and HEAD requests are never capped.\n4. The recipe page's drop zone accepts an image dragged from another site and sends its URL to the new endpoint (the frontend is not graded).\n\nTests: `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests/unit_tests/pkgs/safehttp tests/unit_tests/services_tests tests/integration_tests/user_recipe_tests` for quick loops; the whole backend suite is `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests` (about 8 minutes).",
  "title": "feat: support drag-and-drop images from web (#8493)",
